From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: damnbitimcrit <333091414+damnbitimcrit@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [GPU] Profile the GPU command thread (gpu_thread_profile)

Xenia's GPU command thread limits Halo 3's frame rate in busy scenes at
about 1.7 ms + 2.3 us per draw per frame, and nothing said where that time
goes. With gpu_thread_profile (off by default, checked at every swap so a
title's configuration can turn it on), the thread laps its time into
exclusive buckets that together cover it: idle, the packet stream, shader
loads, sync (WAIT_REG_MEM, host GPU waits, interrupts), each stage of a
draw (setup, primitives, render targets, pipeline, textures, system
constants, bindings, shared memory), resolves, submissions and the swap.

- A lap reads the time stamp counter into plain per-thread variables
  (GpuThreadProfiler, reached through a thread-local pointer that is null
  on other threads and while off); scoped zones return to the bucket they
  interrupted, so nested work (uploads inside texture loads, submissions
  inside the swap) counts where it happens. The thread adds its numbers to
  shared atomics once per swap. A lap's cost is measured when profiling
  starts, for the reader to take out.
- Counts: packets and primary buffers, shader loads, interrupts, draws
  entered, recorded and waiting for a pipeline, pipeline lookups (current,
  found, new), root signature changes, resolves, submissions with the time
  spent replaying the deferred command list (and its command count) and
  closing and executing it, shared memory uploads and bytes, texture loads
  and bytes, render target transfers.
- Halo 3's stats print it as a "Halo 3 GPU profile" line in ms per frame,
  the draw stages in us per draw, against the time stamp counter's
  frequency measured over each interval.
